      <p>These are the items the batch and its review left for you, sorted by your rule of 00:12 UTC today (02:12 yours). A fork that the record's principles settle, meaning the team's later word, the library as the team wrote it, or the peers, the coordinator decides and lists. A fork on the goal, one that would reverse a decision of yours, one where the principles split, or one that a rule of yours reserves comes to you. Nothing here holds the work. Each default is what landed, or what's in place now.</p>

      <h3>For you</h3>
      <p class="pt"><b>The count's one error.</b> Every reduction and comprehension runs through the library function <code>__bigOperator</code>, whose local <code>body(i)</code> gives <code>i</code> no type, and the checker refuses that, as you answered. Writing <code>body(i: I)</code> makes walk refuse list and map comprehensions, because walk gives <code>I</code> the empty type when nothing fixes it. Example: <code>xs = &lt;| 2 k | k &lt;- 0#3 |&gt;</code> prints <code>&lt;|0, 2, 4|&gt;</code> today, and with the type written it stops with &ldquo;body param 1 (i:BOTTOM) got arg 4&rdquo;. Default: the line stays untyped, and the count stays 1. It's yours because the two ways are your open question on walk's plain-bounded parameters, or an untyped <code>fn (i) =&gt; ...</code> that goes against your answer.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b><code>numerator</code> on each integer type, an extension of the library.</b> <code>numerator</code> gives a number's numerator as a <code>ZZ</code>, the unbounded integer, and for an integer it's the integer itself. The checker refused the default body in the trait <code>Integral</code>, so the fix removed it and declared <code>numerator(self): ZZ</code> on <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>NN32</code> and <code>NN64</code>: four new api lines, the declarations in the interface files, copied from <code>ZZ</code>'s own. Example: <code>numerator(7)</code>, with 7 a <code>ZZ32</code>, still answers the <code>ZZ</code> 7. Default: as landed. It's yours by your library rule: you judge each extension, shown the line it copies.</p>
      <h3>Settled, nothing needed from you</h3>
      <p class="pt"><b>Team declarations changed in <code>Random</code> and <code>QuickCheck</code>.</b> Under your coverage answer, walk refuses an object whose bodies don't cover an inherited abstract method, and three library places relied on the old allowance. The fix gave each a body copied from a line of its own file: the seeded distribution wrapper passes <code>seedSize</code> and <code>reseed</code> to the generator it wraps, Mersenne Twister's setup takes <code>perturbed</code> at the abstract declaration's type, <code>Generator[\ZZ32\]</code>, and QuickCheck's any-value generator gets <code>Gen</code>'s default <code>perturb</code>. Example: <code>perturb(obj: Any, g) = g</code>, where before a call outside its one narrower body stopped walk with &ldquo;has neither body nor def&rdquo;. Default: as landed. Your coverage answer asked for exactly these repairs first, and each body copies a line of its own file. The coordinator's call: as landed.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>Walk and a call written as another call's argument.</b> Walk has no static types, so it doesn't give an argument the expected type that the checker now gives it. With <code>mk[\T\](): BoxT[\T\]</code>, <code>takesBox64(mk())</code> runs compiled but stops under walk, and where another declaration accepts <code>Any</code>, walk runs that one, a different answer from the compiled program. Example: under walk, <code>takesBox64(mk())</code> stops with &ldquo;got arg BoxT[\Any\]&rdquo;. Default: walk unchanged, the gap marked by an expected failure. Walk has no static types until the switch-over, when it takes the checker's, as your decision on walk's coercions already says. The coordinator's call: as landed, the gap closed at the switch-over.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>Walk's coverage search when it can't finish.</b> To check coverage, walk splits each parameter type into the cases its <code>comprises</code> list names, up to a limit, and past the limit it now accepts the object, as another of walk's checks does, while the compiled checker refuses it. Your coverage answer makes walk agree with the checker, so the two pull different ways, and no program in the suites, the 62 demos or the specification's examples reaches the limit. Example: a method of five parameters, each of a type with six cases, makes about 7.8K combinations, and walk loads the object that the checker refuses. Default: walk accepts, and an expected failure marks the gap. Your coverage answer says walk agrees with the checker, and the checker refuses. The coordinator's call: walk refuses past the limit too, in a later walk fix; nothing reaches the limit today.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b><code>IntMap</code>'s disjoint join with a one-entry map.</b> <code>UPLUS</code> joins two maps that mustn't share a key, and its comment promises a <code>KeyOverlap</code> error when they do. A one-entry map on the left throws, but on the right the join quietly keeps the many-entry map's value, where the library's own <code>addNew</code> would throw. Example: <code>{0 |-&gt; 0, 1 |-&gt; 1, 2 |-&gt; 2} UPLUS {1 |-&gt; 9}</code> answers the three-entry map unchanged, while the other order throws. Default: today's value, pinned by a test. The library's own <code>addNew</code> throws on a shared key, as the comment promises. The coordinator's call: the one-entry case throws too, in a later library fix, with today's value pinned until then.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>The team's trips demo, refused by the self-type rule.</b> The demo declares <code>object generator(...) extends StandardTotalOrder[\trip\]</code>, but a generator isn't a trip, so the checker now refuses it, while walk still runs the demo and no gated test reads it. A one-line edit to <code>StandardTotalOrder[\generator\]</code> would match the order its own <code>CMP</code> defines. Default: the demo stays as it is. Your word on the demos settles it: leave them. The coordinator's call: as landed, the gap ledger's row recording the refusal.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>The mixin reading of the self-type rule.</b> A trait's parameter, bounded by a self-typed trait that it extends at that parameter, counts as its self parameter, as Scala reads a self-type annotation, so the team's 2009 <code>Containment</code> mixins check clean. That reads the library as the team wrote it. The coordinator's call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>Three readings in the self-type text.</b> A <code>comprises</code> list of only <code>...</code> counts as none, the extender check compares a trait's self type as Scala's conformance rule does, and the text names the new term &ldquo;self-type parameter&rdquo;. Nothing measured moves. The call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>The retry reaches a reduction's big operator.</b> <code>s: ZZ32 = BIG LAST[j &lt;- lo#n] (3 j)</code> now compiles, while one whose parameter's bound names itself, like <code>SUM</code>'s, is still refused. Your answer's words cover it. The call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>The retry's three small choices.</b> A generic call around the retried argument is fixed from its other arguments and its own expected type, a tie is refused with today's message, and a call that writes its own static arguments is retried too. Your answer's definition settles them. The call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>A compiled program's answer changed, and the inference chapter gained a paragraph.</b> <code>mk() OPLUS mk() OPLUS mk()</code> now runs the three-operand declaration, not the two-operand one, as the specification's text on chained operators says, and the paragraph states your answer. The call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>No retry for a call without static arguments of its own.</b> <code>takesBox64(p(mk()))</code>, with <code>p</code> overloaded but not generic, stays refused, as your answer's words limit it. The call: as your answer says.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>A negative exponent in a size, for your sight.</b> Walk now refuses it, and the compiled path computes none; the specification is silent. The call: as built.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>A gap row for walk's refusal of the typed <code>body(i)</code>.</b> The gap ledger is the record's list of known gaps, one row each. Walk's refusal got its own row, beside your open question that covers the same case, rather than being folded into it. The call: the row as filed.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>A suspected type slip in <code>Random</code>'s two distribution wrappers.</b> No stage checks <code>Random</code>, so it isn't measured. The call: no row until it's measured.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>Two of the revival's test pins changed with the repairs.</b> The <code>SYMDIFF</code> test now asserts the right maps, and a test that pinned a refusal now passes, as your answer named. The call: as landed.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>A box in the specification's section on proposed features.</b> A skeptic made it agree with the restated No Duplicates Rule, and no normative text changed. The call: as landed.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>The gap ledger's tool.</b> It can't set a row's test, add a long note or edit an open row's claim, so 12 notes were refused and one row's claim is half false. The call: the coordinator's hand edit, or a route in the tool.</p>
      <p class="pt"><b>Three small debts.</b> The batch script's retired handover lines, a row number owed in the specification's box on dispatch, and a test owed for a body that returns <code>self</code> at a trait type. The call: the coordinator and the next fixes.</p>
